ike the Syriac, mean thing, but hardly, as that does here, fault or crime. The Vat. ms., pointing differently, reads thing. The same confusion occurs at § 40, 35 (cf. a converse case in § 25, 40).","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3476"},{"text":"[^n3477]: So Ciasca’s text, following the Borg. ms. The Vat. ms. has plotted, which is nearer the Syriac accuse.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3477"},{"text":"[^n3478]: Luke xxiii. 15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3478"},{"text":"[^n3479]: Luke xxiii. 16.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3479"},{"text":"[^n3480]: Luke xxiii. 18a.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3480"},{"text":"[^n3481]: Mark xv. 3a.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3481"},{"text":"[^n3482]: Matt. xxvii. 12.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3482"},{"text":"[^n3483]: Matt. xxvii. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3483"},{"text":"[^n3484]: Matt. xxvii. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3484"},{"text":"[^n3485]: Matt. xxvii. 19.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3485"},{"text":"[^n3486]: See § 3, 12, note.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3486"},{"text":"[^n3487]: Matt. xxvii. 15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3487"},{"text":"[^n3488]: Matt. xxvii. 16.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3488"},{"text":"[^n3489]: Matt. xxvii. 17a.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3489"},{"text":"[^n3490]: John xviii. 39.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3490"},{"text":"[^n3491]: John xviii. 40.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3491"},{"text":"[^n3492]: Luke xxiii. 19.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3492"},{"text":"[^n3493]: Ciasca’s text, following the Vat. ms., has disorder. Borg. ms. has division (cf. heresies, Curetonian of § 50, 37), which by addition of a diacritical point gives sedition; cf. § 50, 37 (Ciasca, following Vat. ms.), and Peshitta (both places).","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3493"},{"text":"[^n3494]: Mark xv. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3494"},{"text":"[^n3495]: Mark xv. 9a; Matt. xxvii. 17b.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3495"},{"text":"[^n3496]: Matt. xxvii. 18.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3496"},{"text":"[^n3497]: Matt. xxvii. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3497"},{"text":"[^n3498]: Matt. xxvii. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3498"},{"text":"[^n3499]: Matt. xxvii. 22a.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3499"},{"text":"[^n3500]: Mark xv. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3500"},{"text":"[^n3501]: Luke xxiii. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3501"},{"text":"[^n3502]: Luke xxiii. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3502"},{"text":"[^n3503]: Luke xxiii. 22.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3503"},{"text":"[^n3504]: Our translator has retained the Syriac word, which in this context means fault (see § 50, 11, note).","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3504"},{"text":"[^n3505]: Luke xxiii. 23.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3505"},{"text":"[^n3506]: The word used in Vat ms. means a repeated charge or attack. That in Borg. ms. is probably used in the post-classical sense of importuning him. Either word might be written by a copyist for the other. The same double reading probably occurs again at § 53, 55.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3506"},{"text":"[^n3507]: Mark xv. 15a; Luke xxiii. 25a.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3507"},{"text":"[^n3508]: Matt. xxvii. 26b.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3508"},{"text":"[^n3509]: cf. Syriac versions.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3509"},{"text":"[^n3510]: Matt. xxvii. 27.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3510"},{"text":"[^n3511]: Matt. xxvii. 28.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3511"},{"text":"[^n3512]: John xix. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3512"},{"text":"[^n3513]: Matt. xxvii. 29b.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3513"},{"text":"[^n3514]: This may be a mere clerical error (very natural in Arabic) for scoffed at, the reading of the Syriac versions. This being so, it is worthy of remark that the reading is apparently common to the two mss. The Syriac words are, however, also somewhat similar. The Jerusalem Lectionary has a word agreeing with the text above.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3514"},{"text":"[^n3515]: Lit. Peace.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3515"},{"text":"[^n3516]: Matt. xxvii. 30.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3516"},{"text":"[^n3517]: John xix. 3b.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3517"},{"text":"[^n3518]: John xix. 4.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3518"},{"text":"[^n3519]: This reading may be a corruption of a very literal rendering of the Peshitta.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3519"},{"text":"[^n3520]: cf. § 50, 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3520"},{"text":"[^n3521]: John xix. 5.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3521"},{"text":"[^n3522]: John xix. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3522"},{"text":"[^n3523]: cf. § 11, 11, note.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3523"},{"text":"[^n3524]: See § 50, 35, note.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3524"},{"text":"[^n3525]: John xix. 7.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3525"},{"text":"[^n3526]: John xix. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3526"},{"text":"[^n3527]: John xix. 9.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3527"},{"text":"[^n3528]: John xix. 10.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3528"},{"text":"[^n3529]: Borg. ms., Why speakest; a reading that might be a corruption of the Peshitta.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3529"},{"text":"[^n3530]: John xix. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3530"},{"text":"[^n3531]: Lit. even one (Pesh.).","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3531"},{"text":"[^n3532]: John xix. 12.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3532"},{"text":"[^n3533]: John xix. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3533"},{"text":"[^n3534]: John xix. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3534"},{"text":"[^n3535]: Lit. six hours.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3535"},{"text":"[^n3536]: John xix. 15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3536"},{"text":"[^n3537]: Matt. xxvii. 24.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3537"},{"text":"[^n3538]: Or, that.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n3538"},{"text":"[^n3539]: cf. Peshitta. Or, Ye know (cf. Sinaitic).","kind":"commentary","